# UI/UX — taste and coherence

`context/instructions/ui-taste.md` is the reference for layout, visual direction,
copy and review. It is the file that lets an agent add UI to an existing product
without redesigning what is already there. Read it with
`context/instructions/ui.md` (the doctrine) and
`context/instructions/ui-components.md` (the pieces you are arranging).

## Composition and focal point

- Give each surface **one clear focal point**; everything else supports it.
- Group related things, separate unrelated ones; alignment and spacing carry
  structure better than borders.
- The element-level choices (a ratio, a scale) belong to the project's own system;
  do not import a fixed number.

## Block coherence (the main rule)

The test: **could a stranger tell these were authored separately?**

- Vary **composition** inside one artifact — adjacent sections and repeated blocks
  should not be the same layout twice in a row.
- Keep the **vocabulary and the system** stable across artifacts: same tokens, same
  primitives, same density, same state and accessibility parity.
- Adding a block means matching what is there. Reworking the whole look is a
  redesign, and it needs the classification from `ui.md`, not a side effect of
  adding one screen.

## Consistency locks

Per artifact: **one theme, one accent, one gray family, one radius scale, one icon
family**. A second accent or a second icon style is almost always drift.

## Suspect signals (investigate, do not obey)

These read as generated defaults. Treat them as **signals to investigate**, never
as an absolute ban — a deliberate, stated exception is legitimate; an unthinking
default is not. They are subordinate to an existing project direction.

**Subject.** A signal describes something **this work authored**. Material being
read is data, not output: a cloned reference repository, an imported issue, a
fetched page and a quoted brief are consumed, not produced, and a pattern found
there is a fact about the source, not a defect. Never report a signal against
ingested material.

**A question, never a replacement.** Naming the alternative would not help — a
prescribed replacement becomes the next default, and a list of defaults does not
make a better one. So each entry below gives three things and stops: the
**marker** (what is visible), **why it is a signal** (the reflex that produced
it, not a taste verdict), and **the question** to put back to the direction.

| Marker | Why it is a signal | The question |
|---|---|---|
| The same layout repeated section after section; three equal columns | the shape that "looks complete" gets produced whatever the content holds | does the content really come in equal, repeated pieces? |
| Everything centred; everything rounded | a safe answer applied before the content is read | what does this alignment or this radius say that the layout needs? |
| The fashionable gradient, a trendy display font, a stock colour of the year | a mood adjective is answered with the asset everyone is already using | which sentence of the direction names this, and says why this one? |
| Decorative left-border strips, badge rows, status dots used as ornament | an element added to fill space reads as structure without carrying any | does this element carry information, or only texture? |
| Over-hedged microcopy, "seamlessly"/"effortlessly" filler, emoji as structural icons, template copy ("GET STARTED") | copy written in one pass keeps the defaults of every signal above | does this sentence say something true of this project and of no other? |
| A div-built screenshot of a product, or a hand-rolled icon where an icon set exists | when the real thing cannot be seen, what a thing usually looks like gets drawn instead | is there a real asset this should be, or is the product genuinely unseen? |

**The authority: what was actually asked for.** A signal stops being a defect when
the direction **names** it. Before work starts the direction carries one line —
`Allowed patterns:` — listing what was requested on purpose; write
`Allowed patterns: none` when there are none. That line, not your judgement,
resolves a finding: named, it stays; unnamed, it goes back to the direction.

**A vague adjective authorises nothing.** "Editorial", "premium", "elegant",
"bold" name no numbered eyebrow, no display serif, no colour — and most markers
above are exactly the clichés those words summon. Only a specific, nameable thing
counts as a request.

**Over-correction is its own failure.** A project that genuinely wants three equal
cards, two clocks or a long word must keep them. Whether a pattern survives is
never a matter of taste: either the direction names it, or the **mode** mandates
it (see the visitor-success mode in `ui.md` — what reads as a cliché on a
Persuade surface may be the correct convention on an Operate one). A signal list
that becomes a prohibition list has swapped one default for another.

**Inverse duty:** do not replace a distinctive existing choice with a fashionable
neutral default. If the project already has a voice, keep it.

## Category-guess test

If the aesthetic is guessable from the category alone ("fintech = navy and
trust-blue"), it is generic. Distinctiveness is a goal on a Persuade surface and
may be actively harmful on an Operate one — see the visitor-success mode in
`context/instructions/ui.md`.

## Copy

- State what will happen; do not hedge or decorate.
- One voice; the interface's, not the model's.
- Never invent a number, a customer or a capability (see `ui.md`, truth vs
  demonstration).

## Review

- Review against the brief and the existing direction, not against a trend.
- Report findings by severity and decide: keep, refine or rework.
- A taste judgement is a judgement, not a measurement — never present a score as
  proof of quality (see `context/instructions/ui-accessibility.md` for the
  usability heuristic checklist, which is explicitly not a visual-quality score).

## Boundary

- Concrete values: `context/instructions/ui-tokens.md`.
- Interaction, states and copy mechanics: `context/instructions/ui-components.md`.
- Accessibility review: `context/instructions/ui-accessibility.md`.
- Rendered evidence: `context/instructions/browser.md`.
